{# JLC placement step, once per part: pin 1 and polarity. Non-polarised parts skip without an LLM call. #}
{{ config(each="parts.parts[*]", output_format="json") }}
{% set p = ref('parts') %}
{% if not p.polarised %}
{{ skip_and_set_to_value("not polarised") }}
{% else %}
You are checking one polarised part in JLCPCB's component placement view,
before ordering. On a real board a flyback diode's logical pins were right but
its physical pads were reversed, and it shorted 12 V to ground.

Part {{ p.ref }} ({{ p.function }}), {{ p.value }}, LCSC {{ p.lcsc }}.
Pins and the nets they land on:
{{ p.pins | tojson(indent=2) }}

1. Name the physical marking that identifies pin 1, the cathode or the anode
   in this part's datasheet (band, chamfer, dot, "−").
2. State which net the marked pad must land on in this circuit, and whether the
   pins above agree.
3. Before looking, say what you EXPECT to see in JLC's 3D placement view if the
   part is right, and what would mean it is reversed or rotated. CPL rotation
   is often wrong for SOT-23, diodes and LEDs.
4. Say what to check on the bench before trusting it at power-up.

Return only JSON:
{
  "ref": "{{ p.ref }}",
  "part": "{{ p.value }}",
  "marking": "the datasheet marking for pin 1 / cathode",
  "marked_pad_net": "net the marked pad must be on",
  "pins_agree": true,
  "expect_in_jlc_3d": "...",
  "reversed_if": "...",
  "power_up_check": "...",
  "issues": ["empty when the part is right"]
}
{% endif %}
